  icon: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 4ab77a8c95c6fbb49a1687036b6898f6, type: 3}
  title: 'Lesson: learning how to make projectiles in Unity'
  sections:
  - heading: Hitscan vs traveling projectiles
    text: In fps games those are usually the two ways to create projectiles for guns.
      Hitscans are instant and if something was in your lens when you shoot then
      it is hit. Traveling projectiles take time, will sometimes be physic based
      and will detect collision on their own. They necessitate to be instantiated
      and destroyed.<br>The two methods have their pros and cons, we'll look at both
      in this sample project.
    links: []
    icon: {fileID: 0}
    scene: {fileID: 0}
    checkList: []
  - heading: Traveling projectiles
    text: We instantiate bullets with their own speed and behavior that explode on
      impact.
    links:
    - linkText: Unity's documentation on raycasts
      url: https://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/Physics.Raycast.html
    - linkText: Unity's documentation on LookAt
      url: https://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/Transform.LookAt.html
